ted the mandatory boot-identity restart/recovery protocol on all three managed nodes.\",\"actions_performed\":[\"Bootstrapped pip and Ansible 2.9.27 (with the cryptography module) on node1 since no package repos were configured\",\"Wired passwordless SSH between node1 and node2 using the cluster-provided keypair, and added ansible.cfg so the elevated Ansible run could use it\",\"Wrote an Ansible project (~/ca-tls-ansible on node1) with roles ca_authority (CA key + self-signed cert, node2 key/CSR/signed cert via openssl_privatekey/openssl_csr/openssl_certificate), ca_trust (installs the CA cert into /etc/pki/ca-trust/source/anchors and runs update-ca-trust), and https_service (deploys key+cert chain, a Python TLS HTTP server, a fleet-https.service systemd unit, and opens firewalld 8443/tcp) on node2\",\"Ran the playbook (site.yml) end-to-end idempotently against inventory groups ca=node1 and web=node2\",\"Verified CA subject 'Fleet Internal CA' with CA:TRUE basic constraint, node2 cert chaining to it with correct SAN, and curl with no extra flags trusting https://node2:8443 from both node1 and node2 via the system trust store; confirmed firewalld persists the 8443 rule\",\"Captured healthy baselines, then restarted node1, node2, and node3 one at a time via systemctl reboot, polled each until exec access returned, and proved changed /proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id values plus post-restart recovery (CA/cert files intact, fleet-https auto-started and enabled, firewall rule persisted, cross-node HTTPS still trusted, node3 baseline state unchanged)\"],\"evidence\":[{\"requirement\":\"Create CA 'Fleet Internal CA' on node1\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-155309490f214bc3\",\"cmd-716cc6065a0d4603\",\"cmd-e2fb192765b74b9c\"],\"summary\":\"ca_authority role generates CA key + self-signed cert with CN=Fleet Internal CA and CA:TRUE; playbook run created it; openssl x509 confirms subject and basic constraints on node1\"},{\"requirement\":\"Issue node2 a TLS certificate from that CA\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-155309490f214bc3\",\"cmd-bb6604a23d064ce4\",\"cmd-2e13e7e4a2a84e63\"],\"summary\":\"node2 key/CSR/cert generated and signed by the CA (ownca provider); served cert verified subject=CN=node2, issuer=CN=Fleet Internal CA, SAN=DNS:node2/IP:10.100.75.11\"},{\"requirement\":\"Configure node2 to serve HTTPS on port 8443 with that certificate\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-40ecc36e8e074f9f\",\"cmd-687844ed171c4e2a\",\"cmd-e4012fd44f064c70\",\"cmd-cafe8be3f8d240a1\",\"cmd-760785800c404942\"],\"summary\":\"https_service role deployed cert/key, systemd unit fleet-https, and firewalld 8443/tcp rule; fleet-https active/listening on 8443; cross-node curl from node1 to https://10.100.75.11:8443 succeeded after firewall fix\"},{\"requirement\":\"Install CA as trusted system CA on node1 and node2\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-e2fb192765b74b9c\",\"cmd-99cb9418a6d645a0\",\"cmd-358931f7b7824bc5\",\"cmd-7ae1597f99184978\"],\"summary\":\"CA cert placed in /etc/pki/ca-trust/source/anchors and update-ca-trust run on both nodes; curl with no --cacert flag trusts https://node2:8443 from both node1 (system-wide) and node2 (localhost hostname); trust list shows 'Fleet Internal CA' anchor on node2\"},{\"requirement\":\"Implement using Ansible\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-d60afecffd6d41c0\",\"cmd-6499f5bdf8c9405b\",\"cmd-687844ed171c4e2a\"],\"summary\":\"Ansible 2.9.27 installed and used to run site.yml (roles ca_authority, ca_trust, https_service) against inventory covering node1 and node2, with a clean idempotent second run\"},{\"requirement\":\"node1 restart evidence: baseline, restart, changed boot identity, recovery\",\"command_ids\":[\"cmd-ab41a0f704f841d1\",\"cmd-0e31425ffe0e4b81\",\"cmd-c58bb7a8f4634d48\",\"cmd-d63a5af7a5564362\"],\"summary\":\"Baseline boot_id 192f6cab...; systemctl reboot issued and disconnected; post-reboot boot_id changed to 1fd63285...; CA files, trust anchor, and cross-node HTTPS trust all confirmed intact after restart\"},{\"requirement\":\"node2 restart e